1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,777
Japan.

2
00:00:00,804 --> 00:00:02,295
Nineteen seventy-nine.

3
00:00:02,322 --> 00:00:06,436
Adults patrol the streets, searching for a woman described by

4
00:00:06,463 --> 00:00:07,265
terrified children.

5
00:00:07,292 --> 00:00:08,424
She wears a mask.

6
00:00:08,451 --> 00:00:09,895
She asks one question.

7
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:11,537
But here is the contradiction

8
00:00:11,564 --> 00:00:12,762
the fear was real.

9
00:00:12,789 --> 00:00:15,693
The supernatural suspect was never verified.

10
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,316
One influential origin account begins in rural Gifu, late nineteen

11
00:00:21,344 --> 00:00:21,851
seventy-eight.

12
00:00:21,879 --> 00:00:24,890
An elderly woman was said to have seen a figure

13
00:00:24,919 --> 00:00:27,018
with a mouth stretched toward both ears.

14
00:00:27,046 --> 00:00:29,427
The location varies between accounts.

15
00:00:29,456 --> 00:00:32,692
Even the original witness story remains unconfirmed.

16
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,706
A newspaper reference gives us a firmer anchor.

17
00:00:35,734 --> 00:00:40,826
A National Diet Library entry identifies a January twenty-sixth, nineteen

18
00:00:40,855 --> 00:00:43,899
seventy-nine item in a Gifu newspaper.

19
00:00:43,927 --> 00:00:46,597
That establishes a published reference to the rumor.

20
00:00:46,626 --> 00:00:49,692
It does not establish the existence of the woman herself.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,247
Her description changed with every telling.

22
00:00:52,275 --> 00:00:52,879
Tall.

23
00:00:52,908 --> 00:00:54,029
Long dark hair.

24
00:00:54,057 --> 00:00:56,637
Sometimes a beige coat, sometimes red.

25
00:00:56,665 --> 00:00:58,163
A white surgical mask.

26
00:00:58,192 --> 00:01:02,145
Some versions gave her a height of one hundred seventy-five

27
00:01:02,173 --> 00:01:02,542
centimeters.

28
00:01:02,571 --> 00:01:06,692
None of these measurements identifies a verified individual.

29
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,409
The central encounter was brutally simple.

30
00:01:09,429 --> 00:01:11,328
A woman stops a child and asks

31
00:01:11,348 --> 00:01:13,932
‘Am I pretty?’ If the answer is yes, she removes

32
00:01:13,952 --> 00:01:14,454
her mask.

33
00:01:14,474 --> 00:01:17,210
Beneath it lies a mouth split almost to the ears.

34
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:18,700
Then comes another question.

35
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,174
‘Even like this?’ In the story, neither ordinary answer guarantees

36
00:01:23,194 --> 00:01:23,592
safety.

37
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:24,429
Say no

38
00:01:24,449 --> 00:01:25,600
and she may attack.

39
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:26,470
Say yes

40
00:01:26,490 --> 00:01:28,326
and she may try to give you her face.

41
00:01:28,346 --> 00:01:30,700
The question itself becomes the weapon.

42
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,091
By spring, the story was traveling beyond Gifu.

43
00:01:34,117 --> 00:01:36,063
Children carried it between schools.

44
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:38,747
Families repeated it over telephone lines.

45
00:01:38,773 --> 00:01:41,222
Newspapers and magazines amplified it.

46
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:44,351
Within months, versions circulated across Japan

47
00:01:44,378 --> 00:01:46,693
from northern communities to the far south.

48
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,444
The response moved beyond playground gossip.

49
00:01:49,474 --> 00:01:52,740
Schools and families organized escorted journeys home.

50
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:54,547
Adults conducted patrols.

51
00:01:54,577 --> 00:01:57,252
Some accounts describe increased police presence.

52
00:01:57,282 --> 00:02:01,627
Precautions were being taken against a figure whose identity and

53
00:02:01,658 --> 00:02:04,690
alleged supernatural powers could not be confirmed.

54
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,409
As the tale expanded, so did the monster.

55
00:02:07,429 --> 00:02:09,409
Some children said she carried scissors.

56
00:02:09,429 --> 00:02:10,890
Others described a sickle.

57
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:12,833
Some claimed she could outrun anyone.

58
00:02:12,853 --> 00:02:15,244
Each new detail made the woman more frightening

59
00:02:15,264 --> 00:02:16,700
and harder to disprove.

60
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,222
But where did her mutilated face come from?

61
00:02:19,243 --> 00:02:21,529
Modern retellings offer a much older answer

62
00:02:21,551 --> 00:02:25,284
a samurai, a betrayal, and a punishment carved into flesh.

63
00:02:25,305 --> 00:02:26,947
The story is unforgettable.

64
00:02:26,969 --> 00:02:29,698
Its historical foundation is another matter.

65
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,930
One popular explanation takes us back to feudal Japan.

66
00:02:33,955 --> 00:02:38,231
A beautiful woman lives within a powerful samurai household.

67
00:02:38,256 --> 00:02:40,302
Some versions call her his wife.

68
00:02:40,327 --> 00:02:41,881
Others, his concubine.

69
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:43,192
The details change

70
00:02:43,217 --> 00:02:46,656
but each version begins with beauty treated as something a

71
00:02:46,681 --> 00:02:47,695
man can own.

72
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,625
The tale says jealousy consumed the samurai.

73
00:02:50,646 --> 00:02:52,451
Perhaps he suspected an affair.

74
00:02:52,472 --> 00:02:54,468
Perhaps the accusation was enough.

75
00:02:54,489 --> 00:02:57,574
What matters is the punishment he supposedly chose.

76
00:02:57,595 --> 00:02:59,572
He would not simply reject the woman.

77
00:02:59,592 --> 00:03:02,699
He would destroy the feature by which others admired her.

78
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,503
In the most brutal retelling, the blade tears across her

79
00:03:06,525 --> 00:03:06,855
face.

80
00:03:06,878 --> 00:03:10,280
Her mouth becomes a wound extending toward the ears.

81
00:03:10,302 --> 00:03:14,378
The samurai then demands an impossible answer: who could ever

82
00:03:14,401 --> 00:03:16,018
call her beautiful now?

83
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:18,698
This is the legend's central act of cruelty.

84
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,530
Now consider what the story is really doing.

85
00:03:21,552 --> 00:03:24,656
It turns violence into a question about appearance.

86
00:03:24,679 --> 00:03:28,511
The wounded woman is denied control over her own face.

87
00:03:28,533 --> 00:03:32,930
Her imagined revenge begins with forcing strangers to repeat the

88
00:03:32,952 --> 00:03:34,698
judgment that destroyed her.

89
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,971
Japanese ghost traditions include the onryō

90
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,348
a spirit driven by intense resentment.

91
00:03:40,378 --> 00:03:44,340
In this interpretation, Kuchisake-onna fits the pattern.

92
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:47,461
A violent wrong produces a relentless haunting.

93
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:51,792
But that resemblance is literary analysis, not proof that a

94
00:03:51,822 --> 00:03:55,691
real murdered woman became the monster of nineteen seventy-nine.

95
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,831
Here the investigation reaches a missing link.

96
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:04,089
No reliably identified samurai, concubine, crime date, or surviving case

97
00:04:04,118 --> 00:04:09,349
record establishes this episode as the beginning of the modern

98
00:04:09,378 --> 00:04:09,874
legend.

99
00:04:09,903 --> 00:04:13,330
We have a compelling explanation for the monster's appearance

100
00:04:13,359 --> 00:04:16,691
but not a demonstrated chain of historical events.

101
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,243
Older Japanese ghost literature also contains disturbing images of women

102
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:23,824
with abnormal mouths.

103
00:04:23,853 --> 00:04:25,213
Those motifs matter.

104
00:04:25,242 --> 00:04:28,794
They show that the visual idea was not entirely new.

105
00:04:28,823 --> 00:04:32,248
Yet resemblance alone cannot establish direct descent.

106
00:04:32,277 --> 00:04:36,783
The masked woman asking children about beauty is a distinctly

107
00:04:36,813 --> 00:04:37,691
modern configuration.

108
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,800
Other versions blame failed cosmetic surgery.

109
00:04:40,825 --> 00:04:42,781
Some tell of personal betrayal.

110
00:04:42,806 --> 00:04:45,009
Others offer no human past at all.

111
00:04:45,034 --> 00:04:48,801
Every explanation tries to solve the same mystery: why would

112
00:04:48,826 --> 00:04:52,592
a woman's mouth become the center of an endless interrogation?

113
00:04:52,617 --> 00:04:55,695
None has been confirmed as the original cause.

114
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,648
The modern surgical mask changes everything.

115
00:04:58,671 --> 00:05:00,351
It makes the threat ordinary.

116
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:04,118
A masked passerby would not necessarily seem unusual.

117
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:08,078
Only when she removes it does the familiar become impossible.

118
00:05:08,101 --> 00:05:11,790
The legend exploits that tiny interval between recognition

119
00:05:11,813 --> 00:05:12,696
and terror.

120
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,829
The feudal explanation offers a villain, a victim, and a

121
00:05:16,853 --> 00:05:17,214
curse.

122
00:05:17,238 --> 00:05:22,190
But it cannot explain why the story suddenly overwhelmed Japanese

123
00:05:22,214 --> 00:05:24,180
schoolchildren in nineteen seventy-nine.

124
00:05:24,204 --> 00:05:26,950
For that, we must leave the samurai behind

125
00:05:26,973 --> 00:05:29,696
and examine the society that carried the rumor.

126
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,555
Late-nineteen-seventies Japan was changing.

127
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:36,631
Cities and suburbs were linked by expanding routines of work,

128
00:05:36,656 --> 00:05:37,854
commuting, and education.

129
00:05:37,879 --> 00:05:41,591
For children, the evening could mean another journey after regular

130
00:05:41,616 --> 00:05:41,965
school.

131
00:05:41,990 --> 00:05:45,733
The danger they imagined was not inside an ancient castle.

132
00:05:45,758 --> 00:05:47,695
It waited along a familiar road.

133
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,426
The expression kagi-kko described children carrying house keys and spending

134
00:05:52,453 --> 00:05:55,098
time without a parent immediately present.

135
00:05:55,125 --> 00:05:59,693
Changing family schedules made childhood independence a subject of concern.

136
00:05:59,719 --> 00:06:02,577
It is tempting to blame parental absence for the legend

137
00:06:02,603 --> 00:06:06,694
but that is an interpretation, not a demonstrated universal cause.

138
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,849
The stronger explanation lies in the cram school

139
00:06:09,871 --> 00:06:10,663
juku.

140
00:06:10,685 --> 00:06:12,822
Students arrived from different neighborhoods.

141
00:06:12,844 --> 00:06:14,950
One child described the masked woman.

142
00:06:14,973 --> 00:06:17,306
Another carried the story into a second school.

143
00:06:17,328 --> 00:06:20,920
Soon, separate communities were exchanging the same frightening image

144
00:06:20,942 --> 00:06:22,698
while adding details of their own.

145
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,649
After lessons, students walked through streets that looked different after

146
00:06:26,679 --> 00:06:27,018
dark.

147
00:06:27,047 --> 00:06:31,327
An unfamiliar adult, a distant movement, or an ordinary masked

148
00:06:31,356 --> 00:06:33,912
stranger could become a frightening possibility.

149
00:06:33,941 --> 00:06:39,988
Iikura suggests that anxieties about dangerous strangers were projected onto

150
00:06:40,017 --> 00:06:40,596
Kuchisake-onna.

151
00:06:40,625 --> 00:06:43,691
The monster gave those uncertainties a visible shape.

152
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,571
Rumors mutate because people do not repeat them perfectly.

153
00:06:47,593 --> 00:06:49,224
One child mentions a mask.

154
00:06:49,247 --> 00:06:50,546
Another adds scissors.

155
00:06:50,568 --> 00:06:53,452
A third claims the woman can run faster than any

156
00:06:53,475 --> 00:06:53,743
athlete.

157
00:06:53,766 --> 00:06:56,321
The changes make the warning easier to remember

158
00:06:56,344 --> 00:06:59,698
and more difficult to distinguish from something actually witnessed.

159
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,239
The newspapers did not have to invent the apparition to

160
00:07:03,263 --> 00:07:04,219
accelerate its spread.

161
00:07:04,242 --> 00:07:06,869
Children carried stories into public discussion.

162
00:07:06,893 --> 00:07:09,868
Media reports made them visible to new audiences.

163
00:07:09,891 --> 00:07:12,235
Those audiences repeated the warnings.

164
00:07:12,259 --> 00:07:13,546
A feedback loop formed

165
00:07:13,569 --> 00:07:16,696
with fear functioning as its own distribution system.

166
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,506
Adults responded because children's fear demanded attention.

167
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:24,232
Patrols and escorted walks made practical sense as precautions.

168
00:07:24,259 --> 00:07:27,818
But official concern about safety should not be mistaken for

169
00:07:27,844 --> 00:07:29,611
official confirmation of a ghost.

170
00:07:29,637 --> 00:07:33,413
A community can respond to a threat report even when

171
00:07:33,439 --> 00:07:35,694
the report's central claim remains unverified.

172
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,784
Stories about human imitators circulated too.

173
00:07:38,808 --> 00:07:43,161
A secondary account describes an alleged arrest in Himeji in

174
00:07:43,184 --> 00:07:44,035
nineteen seventy-nine.

175
00:07:44,059 --> 00:07:47,251
The original arrest record has not been established here.

176
00:07:47,275 --> 00:07:50,737
Even if that report is accurate, it concerns a human

177
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:51,085
imitator

178
00:07:51,108 --> 00:07:52,696
not proof of the apparition.

179
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,315
The panic did not remain at its peak forever.

180
00:07:55,338 --> 00:08:00,212
On June fifteenth, nineteen seventy-nine, a Gifu newspaper described the

181
00:08:00,235 --> 00:08:02,660
disturbance as finally subsiding locally.

182
00:08:02,684 --> 00:08:06,260
As summer holidays approached, the wider rumor also weakened.

183
00:08:06,284 --> 00:08:09,696
Yet declining fear did not erase the character from memory.

184
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,109
This is the phenomenon's most revealing contradiction.

185
00:08:13,137 --> 00:08:17,717
No verified supernatural attacker was necessary for the legend to

186
00:08:17,744 --> 00:08:18,638
alter behavior.

187
00:08:18,666 --> 00:08:20,698
Ordinary objects became frightening.

188
00:08:20,726 --> 00:08:22,201
Streets gained new meanings.

189
00:08:22,229 --> 00:08:23,685
Parents changed routines.

190
00:08:23,713 --> 00:08:26,677
The woman with the torn mouth had become something larger

191
00:08:26,705 --> 00:08:27,875
than an alleged sighting

192
00:08:27,903 --> 00:08:29,692
a shared expectation of danger.

193
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,405
Return to the encounter.

194
00:08:31,427 --> 00:08:32,814
The woman blocks the road.

195
00:08:32,835 --> 00:08:33,641
She asks

196
00:08:33,663 --> 00:08:36,587
‘Am I pretty?’ It sounds like an ordinary question.

197
00:08:36,609 --> 00:08:40,112
But within the story, your answer determines what happens next.

198
00:08:40,134 --> 00:08:42,698
The conversation has already become a trap.

199
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,222
If you say yes, she removes her mask.

200
00:08:45,242 --> 00:08:47,207
Her mouth stretches toward her ears.

201
00:08:47,227 --> 00:08:48,847
Then comes the second question

202
00:08:48,867 --> 00:08:51,839
‘Even like this?’ The first answer was never enough.

203
00:08:51,858 --> 00:08:54,700
The rules change after you have committed yourself.

204
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,137
Say yes again, and in some versions she marks your

205
00:08:58,161 --> 00:08:59,402
face to resemble hers.

206
00:08:59,425 --> 00:09:01,097
Agreeing does not protect you.

207
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:04,973
Instead, the legend transforms your politeness into permission for the

208
00:09:04,996 --> 00:09:05,748
next horror.

209
00:09:05,771 --> 00:09:08,697
Even the apparently safe answer becomes dangerous.

210
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,217
Say no, and other versions threaten an immediate attack.

211
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:15,866
The story defeats the normal instinct to choose between honesty

212
00:09:15,889 --> 00:09:16,595
and agreement.

213
00:09:16,619 --> 00:09:20,408
A child confronted with the question is given no reliable

214
00:09:20,431 --> 00:09:20,789
answer

215
00:09:20,812 --> 00:09:22,697
and no reliable way to escape.

216
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,751
Some versions offer a loophole.

217
00:09:24,778 --> 00:09:25,442
Answer

218
00:09:25,469 --> 00:09:27,602
‘So-so.’ Neither beautiful nor ugly.

219
00:09:27,628 --> 00:09:30,297
The ambiguous reply interrupts the forced choice.

220
00:09:30,324 --> 00:09:32,860
This is not verified survival advice.

221
00:09:32,887 --> 00:09:37,026
It is a storytelling device that lets the listener imagine

222
00:09:37,053 --> 00:09:38,693
escaping an impossible situation.

223
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,041
Another variation gives the child a sweet: bekkōame, traditional amber-colored

224
00:09:44,067 --> 00:09:45,054
hard candy.

225
00:09:45,081 --> 00:09:48,331
Offering it supposedly distracts the woman long enough to flee.

226
00:09:48,357 --> 00:09:49,818
The detail seems absurd

227
00:09:49,845 --> 00:09:53,301
until you understand that children were creating rules that restored

228
00:09:53,327 --> 00:09:54,693
a feeling of control.

229
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,606
Then there is the strangest escape: pomade.

230
00:09:57,634 --> 00:10:00,976
Say the word three times, some versions insist, and she

231
00:10:01,004 --> 00:10:01,312
retreats.

232
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:04,525
Pomade is a hair-styling product, not a chemical spell.

233
00:10:04,554 --> 00:10:08,741
The arbitrary rule works because folklore allows its audience to

234
00:10:08,769 --> 00:10:11,692
invent weaknesses for an otherwise unbeatable monster.

235
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,391
Notice the contradiction.

236
00:10:13,416 --> 00:10:16,806
The same ghost has different weaknesses in different neighborhoods.

237
00:10:16,831 --> 00:10:19,828
The supposedly unbreakable rules were never fixed.

238
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:22,039
They were revised as the story traveled.

239
00:10:22,064 --> 00:10:24,144
The monster was assembled collectively

240
00:10:24,169 --> 00:10:26,695
and every storyteller could change the machinery.

241
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,773
Decades later, Kuchisake-onna survives in films, games, illustrations, and online

242
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:33,353
stories.

243
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:35,166
The delivery system has changed.

244
00:10:35,193 --> 00:10:36,564
The mechanism has not.

245
00:10:36,591 --> 00:10:38,869
A frightening image enters a community.

246
00:10:38,896 --> 00:10:42,056
People repeat it, reshape it, and give it new reasons

247
00:10:42,082 --> 00:10:42,693
to remain.

248
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,027
The evidence does not establish a supernatural killer.

249
00:10:46,055 --> 00:10:47,997
It establishes something more human

250
00:10:48,025 --> 00:10:50,780
fear, repetition, and collective imagination.

251
00:10:50,808 --> 00:10:54,565
In nineteen seventy-nine, Japan gave those forces a face.

252
00:10:54,594 --> 00:10:57,882
And behind that face was a question nobody wanted to

253
00:10:57,911 --> 00:10:58,214
answer.

254
00:10:58,242 --> 00:10:59,692
The Unsolved Archive.
